 {:db/id "808dcba0-1983-52e5-9209-409e7c477a50"
  :question/id #uuid "808dcba0-1983-52e5-9209-409e7c477a50"
  :question/problem "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 1](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_4/1).\n\nFind the general solution to $y''+9y'-10y=0$. Use $c_1,c_2$ for arbitrary constants and $x$ as the independent variable. In WeBWorK enter the full equation $y=\\cdots$, typing the constants as `c1` and `c2`.\n\nEquation: $y=$ {{solution}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["d6d41ef7-8901-5302-aa8d-adc1a1921758"]
  :question/worked-solution "The characteristic equation is $r^2+9r-10=(r-1)(r+10)=0$. Its distinct roots $1,-10$ give $y=c_1e^x+c_2e^{-10x}$. Each exponential has zero residual when substituted into the ODE."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
