  :question/id #uuid "3b8205f1-49f5-5f5b-8233-53329f239bfe"
  :question/problem "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 4](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_4/4).\n\nFind the general solution to $3y''+75y=0$. Use $c_1,c_2$ for arbitrary constants and $x$ as the independent variable. In WeBWorK enter the full equation $y=\\cdots$, typing the constants as `c1` and `c2`.\n\nEquation: $y=$ {{solution}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["b7ee7b6a-d428-5366-b606-7d43663618fe"]
  :question/worked-solution "Dividing by $3$ gives $y''+25y=0$. The roots $r=\\pm5i$ yield the real solution $y=c_1\\cos(5x)+c_2\\sin(5x)$; differentiating twice multiplies either term by $-25$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
