 {:db/id "ee194d6c-d21a-5cb4-a14d-555dcea2e811"
  :question/id #uuid "ee194d6c-d21a-5cb4-a14d-555dcea2e811"
  :question/problem "Find $W(t)=W(y_1,y_2)$.\n\n$W(t)=$ {{solution}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["118b0aee-d5ec-5adf-80eb-2e7f517442af"]
  :question/worked-solution "Directly, $y_1'=3(e^{3t}-e^{-t/3})/10$ and $y_2'=(9e^{3t}+e^{-t/3})/10$. Thus $W=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2=e^{3t}e^{-t/3}=e^{8t/3}$. For an independent check, normalize the ODE to $y''-(8/3)y'-y=0$. Abel's identity gives $W=Ce^{8t/3}$, and the initial data give $W(0)=1$, hence $C=1$. The Wronskian is nonzero for all real $t$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
