 {:db/id "7628824f-381b-584a-ad74-c603fca83c01"
  :question/id #uuid "7628824f-381b-584a-ad74-c603fca83c01"
  :question/problem "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 8](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_4/8).\n\nFind $y(x)$ satisfying $x^2y''+5xy'-21y=0$, $y(1)=-9$, and $y'(1)=-3$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{solution}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["fb068b1f-c522-5d19-bc64-756037577628"]
  :question/worked-solution "Use $y=x^r$. The indicial equation is $r(r-1)+5r-21=(r-3)(r+7)=0$, so $y=Ax^3+Bx^{-7}$. The conditions give $A+B=-9$ and $3A-7B=-3$, hence $A=-33/5$, $B=-12/5$. The maximal nonsingular interval containing $x=1$ is $x>0$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
