 {:db/id "620c8a43-e919-52fc-8aa0-1bbc270dd594"
  :question/id #uuid "620c8a43-e919-52fc-8aa0-1bbc270dd594"
  :question/problem "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 9](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_4/9).\n\nFind $y(x)$ satisfying $x^2y''+7xy'+9y=0$, $y(1)=-6$, and $y'(1)=1$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{solution}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["5477d5f7-7968-51c0-8d6d-8ce160d06800"]
  :question/worked-solution "The indicial equation is $r(r-1)+7r+9=(r+3)^2=0$. Thus $y=x^{-3}(A+B\\ln x)$ on $x>0$. The value condition gives $A=-6$; the derivative at $1$ is $-3A+B=1$, giving $B=-17$. The interval $x>0$ contains the initial point and avoids the singular point $0$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
