 {:db/id "c25b0f5b-f78e-5dbb-9097-b19d9a3fdbcb"
  :question/id #uuid "c25b0f5b-f78e-5dbb-9097-b19d9a3fdbcb"
  :question/problem "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 16](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_4/16).\n\nUse the method of undetermined coefficients to find one solution of $y''+3y'-3y=(-t^2+9t+5)e^{3t}$. The method is to produce a specific solution, not the general solution.\n\n$y(t)=$ {{solution}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["14a4e964-2121-5be9-a38c-6eaf256499c4"]
  :question/worked-solution "Use the undetermined-coefficients trial $y=e^{3t}(At^2+Bt+C)$. Put $v=At^2+Bt+C$; substitution reduces the left side to $e^{3t}(v''+9v'+15v)$. Matching coefficients gives $15A=-1$, $18A+15B=9$, and $2A+9B+15C=5$. Therefore $A=-1/15$, $B=17/25$, $C=-74/1125$, so $y=e^{3t}(-t^2/15+17t/25-74/1125)$. Direct differentiation verifies the original equation."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
