  :question/id #uuid "c9a45dfc-743a-532d-952a-320e6b25755b"
  :question/problem "Find the most general solution to the original nonhomogeneous equation. Use $c_1,c_2$ for arbitrary constants.\n\n$y=$ {{solution}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["497899bf-1b8e-5e82-a1dc-3bcc8b5f561c"]
  :question/worked-solution "Add a particular solution to the complete homogeneous solution: $y=y_h+y_p$. For the polynomial forcing use $Ax+B$: $5A=-10$, $4A+5B=0$, giving $A=-2$, $B=8/5$. For $3e^{-x}$ use $Ce^{-x}$: $(1-4+5)C=3$, giving $C=3/2$. Add the two particular solutions by linearity. The characteristic polynomial is $(r+2)^2+1$, so the roots $-2\\pm i$ give $y_h=e^{-2x}(c_1\\cos x+c_2\\sin x)$."
