 {:db/id "dc448596-9744-57e6-90a4-9cbde7827d8f"
  :question/id #uuid "dc448596-9744-57e6-90a4-9cbde7827d8f"
  :question/problem "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 18](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_4/18).\n\nFind a particular solution to $6y''+5y'+y=2t^2+2t-5e^{3t}$.\n\n$y_p(t)=$ {{solution}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["6326d70d-acfd-5041-bbab-03590268f54b"]
  :question/worked-solution "Use $y_p=At^2+Bt+C+De^{3t}$. Polynomial coefficients give $A=2$, $10A+B=2$, and $12A+5B+C=0$, hence $B=-18$, $C=66$. The exponential coefficient gives $(54+15+1)D=-5$, so $D=-1/14$. Adding these terms produces the required forcing by linearity."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
